 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "Four families of verbs",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "Every verb in Chapter 1 went like <em>porto</em>. Latin has four families of verbs, called <b>conjugations</b>. They all use the person endings you already know. What changes is the vowel in front of the ending."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus dominum <u class=\"c\">mone</u><u class=\"v\">t</u>.",
      "en": "The slave <b>warns</b> the master.",
      "note": "<em>-t</em> is still \"he\" or \"she\". The <em>e</em> shows that <em>moneo</em> is 2nd conjugation."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "1st conjugation verbs have <em>a</em>: <em>portas</em>. 2nd have <em>e</em>: <em>mones</em>.",
      "3rd conjugation verbs have <em>i</em>, and <em>u</em> in \"they\": <em>trahis</em>, <em>trahunt</em>.",
      "4th conjugation verbs keep an <em>i</em> all the way through: <em>audis</em>, <em>audiunt</em>.",
      "Whatever the vowel, the last letters still tell you the person."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
